        "acc-ingredients": {
          "type": "accordion",
          "settings": {
            "show_below_gallery": true,
            "icon": "none",
            "custom_icon": "",
            "title": "What's in it",
            "content": "<p>Both lists in full, exactly as they read on the jars.</p><p><strong>Restore Balm</strong><br>Petrolatum, Vitis Vinifera (Grape) Seed Oil, Paraffinum Liquidum (Mineral Oil), Cera Alba (Beeswax), Microcrystalline Wax, Chamomilla Recutita (Matricaria) Flower Oil, Lavandula Angustifolia (Lavender) Oil, Curcuma Longa Root Oil, Vanilla Planifolia Fruit Extract.</p><p><strong>Recovery Cream</strong><br>Aqua (Water), Glycerin, Propylene Glycol, Paraffinum Liquidum (Mineral Oil), Caprylic/Capric Triglyceride, Butyrospermum Parkii (Shea) Butter, Cera Alba (Beeswax), Cetearyl Alcohol, Dimethicone, Glyceryl Stearate, PEG-100 Stearate, PEG-20 Stearate, Panthenol, Phenoxyethanol, Methylparaben, Tocopherol, Sodium Hyaluronate, Xanthan Gum, Portulaca Oleracea Extract, Aloe Barbadensis Leaf Extract, Chrysanthellum Indicum Extract, Houttuynia Cordata Extract, Paeonia Suffruticosa Root Bark Extract, Opuntia Streptacantha Stem Extract, Scutellaria Baicalensis Root Extract, Butylene Glycol, 1,2-Hexanediol, Hydroxyacetophenone, Vanilla Planifolia Fruit Extract, CI 19140.</p><p><strong>The balm is built on petrolatum, and that is deliberate.</strong> Nothing holds moisture in better while fresh work closes over. What ruins tattoos is a thick layer, a bare tub and never stepping down — not the petrolatum itself. The full reasoning is in <a href=\"/pages/healing-guide\">The Healing Guide</a>.</p><p>The cream carries the conditioning half: glycerin and sodium hyaluronate to hold water, shea butter and panthenol to soften and comfort, tocopherol, and a botanical set led by purslane, aloe, houttuynia and baicalensis.</p>",
            "liquid": "",
            "page": ""
          }
